     CROND(8)		  UNIX 5.0 (6 March 1987)	      CROND(8)     NAME	  crond	- daemon to execute scheduled commands (Vixie's	Cron)     SYNOPSIS	  crond     DESCRIPTION	  Crond	should be started from /etc/rc or /etc/rc.local.  It	  will return immediately, so you don't	need to	start it with	  '&'.	  Crond	searches for a crontab file for	each user listed in	  /etc/passwd; those crontabs found are	loaded into memory.	  Crond	then wakes up every minute, examining all stored	  crontabs, checking each command to see if it should be run	  in the current minute.  When executing commands, any output	  is mailed to the owner of the	crontab	(or to the user	named	  in the MAILTO	environment variable, if such exists).	  Additionally,	crond checks each minute for the existence of	  a POKECRON file in it's spool	directory.  If it exists, it	  is removed, and crond	will reload its	memory-resident	cron	  tables.  Crontab(1) creates the POKECRON file	whenever it	  makes	a change to any	crontab.     SEE ALSO	  crontab(1), crontab(5)     AUTHOR	  Paul Vixie	  ucbvax!dual!ptsfa!vixie!paul	  ptsfa!vixie!paul@ames.ARPA     Page 1					     (printed 2/10/88)